{"seeAlsoSections":[{"generated":true,"title":"Explanation","anchor":"Explanation","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PluginArchitecture","doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/DesignPrinciples","doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SecurityPosture"]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"Overview","text":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Swidux relies on specific patterns to ensure correct ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" behavior, efficient rendering, and safe concurrency. This article explains the reasoning behind these patterns.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"The-Snapshot-Pattern","text":"The Snapshot Pattern","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"Store.send()","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" copies the observer tree into a local struct, mutates the copy via the reducer, then assigns changed properties back. This happens internally via "},{"code":"SwiduxObservable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State(observer:)"},{"type":"text","text":" packs, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State.apply(_:to:)"},{"type":"text","text":" unpacks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The pattern exists for two reasons:"}]},{"items":[{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" equality checking.","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" The ","type":"text"},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" accessor checks ","type":"text"},{"code":"Equatable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and suppresses no-op notifications. Swift’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"_modify","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" accessor (used by ","type":"text"},{"code":"inout","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") fires notifications unconditionally. The snapshot pattern routes through ","type":"text"},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Cross-slice observation isolation.","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" The ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Swidux","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" macro generates a separate ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" class for each ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Slice","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" property. A view reading ","type":"text"},{"code":"store.items","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" won’t re-render when only ","type":"text"},{"code":"store.ui","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" changes, because they live on different observer objects.","type":"text"}]}]}],"type":"orderedList"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Swidux"},{"text":" macro generates the observer class tree and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SwiduxObservable"},{"text":" conformance automatically. Hand-written conformance is still possible for advanced cases — see ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SwiduxObservable"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"style":"note","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Explicit equality guards ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"if x != state.x { x = state.x }"},{"type":"text","text":") are unnecessary. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"type":"text","text":" already checks equality on "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"set"},{"type":"text","text":". Unconditional assignment is safe."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"type":"aside","name":"Note"},{"anchor":"Dispatch-Loop-Detection","text":"Dispatch Loop Detection","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PersistencePlugin","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"text":" warns if more than 100 changes are drained within a single debounce interval (the ","type":"text"},{"code":"loopThreshold","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" parameter; 250 ms by default). It counts only dispatches that changed an ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/EntityStore","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"text":", and it reports once per burst — the count starts over after a whole debounce interval with no drains. This usually means an effect or plugin dispatches an action on every state change, feeding the cycle it reacts to. A steady stream of edits, such as a slider drag, stays well under the threshold and is not reported.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"Debounced-Persistence","text":"Debounced Persistence","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Each drain restarts the flush’s debounce timer, so a burst of edits is written once. The timer is never pushed further than ","type":"text"},{"code":"maxWait","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" past the oldest pending change (four debounce intervals, and at least a second, by default), so continuous edits still reach storage while they continue. A retry of a failed flush keeps its own schedule; a new edit postpones it only when the flush it schedules is due first and carries the failed batch anyway.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"Reducer-Weight","text":"Reducer Weight","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Reducers run synchronously on the MainActor. Move O(n²) work into an ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/Effect","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"text":" and dispatch the result back as an action.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"Effect-Threading","text":"Effect Threading","type":"heading","level":2},{"style":"important","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Effects run on Swift concurrency’s cooperative thread pool via "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task { @concurrent in }"},{"type":"text","text":". Blocking calls ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Process.waitUntilExit()"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DispatchSemaphore.wait()"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Thread.sleep()"},{"type":"text","text":") hold threads hostage. If enough block, the pool starves and the MainActor freezes."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"type":"aside","name":"Important"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Use async alternatives: ","type":"text"},{"code":"terminationHandler","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" + continuation instead of ","type":"text"},{"code":"waitUntilExit()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"Task.sleep()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" instead of ","type":"text"},{"code":"Thread.sleep()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", async file I\/O instead of ","type":"text"},{"code":"Data(contentsOf:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]}]}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/swidux\/architectureguide"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/ArchitectureGuide","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"sections":[],"kind":"article","metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"Swidux"}],"roleHeading":"Article","role":"article","title":"Architecture & Performance"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux"]]},"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"abstract":[{"text":"Understand the architectural patterns that make Swidux work correctly with SwiftUI’s observation system.","type":"text"}],"references":{"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/SecurityPosture":{"url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/securityposture","abstract":[{"text":"What Swidux’s soft controls actually guarantee, and what to do when you need a hard one.","type":"text"}],"role":"collectionGroup","type":"topic","kind":"article","title":"Security Posture","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SecurityPosture"},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux":{"url":"\/documentation\/swidux","abstract":[{"tex